\section*{Parity of 2-Primary Tate–Shafarevich Groups}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 002; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that for any prime, a rational elliptic curve of Selmer corank zero or one has the corresponding analytic and rational-point ranks, together with finite Tate--Shafarevich group; the application includes primes congruent to $4$, $7$, or $8$ modulo $9$ as sums of two rational cubes.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For every elliptic curve $E/\mathbb Q$, every prime $p$ and $r\in\{0,1\}$, let $s_p(E)$ be the $\mathbb Z_p$-corank of its $p^{\infty}$ Selmer group. The claimed converse is
\[
 s_p(E)=r\ \Longrightarrow\ \operatorname{ord}_{s=1}L(E,s)=\operatorname{rank}E(\mathbb Q)=r,
 \qquad\#\operatorname{Sha}(E/\mathbb Q)<\infty.
\]
No additional reduction, CM, residual-representation, rational-torsion or isogeny hypothesis is imposed. Coranks greater than one and an exact BSD leading coefficient are not concluded by this theorem.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 002; 2026-09-24), a tame deformation gives an upper bound on an Artin-module length. Integral theta-series coefficients and Heegner logarithms produce an incompatible lower bound unless the low-corank converse holds; auxiliary quadratic fields separate the two root-number cases.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The arithmetic conclusions require a rational elliptic curve with Selmer corank zero or one at the specified prime; the rational-two-torsion companion has that additional hypothesis. Rank equality, finite Tate--Shafarevich group, or the stated leading-coefficient identities in those cases do not extend to all ranks and number fields in this problem. Average conclusions for quadratic twists also leave individual exceptional curves untreated.

\subsection{Formal verification}
